CLUE — whole-project Production readiness assessment

Assess the current CLUE candidate and answer: “Are we ready to deploy this exact candidate to Production?” Review the application, release package, installer, provider integrations and operational handoff. This is an assessment only. Use one agent, work sequentially, and write the report in English.

1. Establish the exact candidate and current requirements
Read AGENTS.md, 00_START_HERE.md, 01_CURRENT_STATE.json, the latest deployment/runbook and acceptance documents, and CLUE_DEV_Excel_Run_and_Reuse_Procedure_2026-10-01.md. Locate the latest installer assessment and subsequent repair/test results if available. A repair prompt is not proof that a repair was implemented.

Locate TD-Universe/W001CLUEinitialRepo and verify its actual current branch and full HEAD; do not assume release-candidate/clue-app-clue-20260926 is still the assessed candidate. Record local changes, remote synchronization evidence, candidate version, archive path, SHA-256 and build provenance. If there is no identified Production candidate, say so. Compare source, packaged files and installed files using existing authorized read-only access. Explain generated/configuration differences without reading or printing secrets. Missing host or artifact access is an evidence gap.

2. Verify application and business correctness
Trace the real flow: Excel input -> cheque selection -> Symcor retrieval -> front/back images -> Tungsten OCR -> enriched Excel output.
Check:
- All original worksheets and source A:W values are preserved, including account leading zeros. Exactly one result worksheet is added, with 41 columns A:AO, images at X:Y and eight field/confidence pairs at Z:AO, against the approved current contract.
- Source-row/account/date/ISN/DocID provenance and source-to-cheque association are correct. Do not treat transaction UTI as a unique cheque identifier or invent parent/child filtering.
- CREDIT and DEBIT acceptance have separate evidence, including multiple children, no match, unavailable images, partial OCR and duplicate inputs. Recheck historical Debit NO_MATCH and unavailable-image cases.
- OCR values, confidence scale and image-side attribution meet documented acceptance criteria.
Inspect actual output workbook bytes, manifests, verify.json and comparison.json when available. Distinguish measured counts from expected counts; the small CREDIT baseline expected 27 results, which is not a measured final count. A regression match is not independent proof of correct business mapping. An exit code of 0 alone is not business acceptance.

3. Verify deployment, configuration and secrets
Review download/local-package selection, trusted digest verification before extraction, safe archive members, Linux/Python dependencies, first install, repeat install, upgrade, interruption and rollback.
Verify installer locking before mutation, backup/recovery protection, preservation of existing configuration/data, and failure handling for stage-limited runs. Inspect --only verify statically before considering any execution: historical assessment reported cleanup that could remove an existing tree when backup was skipped.
Recheck the four reported DEV verification failures and five local preinstall failures against actual transcripts and current code. Identify each as FIXED, STILL_PRESENT or UNVERIFIED; do not assume the requested repairs happened.
Trace active environment/profile selection and persisted install/data paths through the actual runtime loader. Resolve the supported environments from current documentation and code. Simulation must default off and be prohibited in server environments; laptop simulation requires explicit configuration, with no automatic fallback.
Verify installer identity versus runtime NPID, scoped privilege delegation for the actual commands, effective file/directory permissions, and the Salt/Pillar/Vault or approved fallback path. Check non-interactive secret retrieval, certificate/key pairing, CA trust, failure behavior, temporary-file cleanup and redacted diagnostics. DEV identities, hosts or endpoints must not silently carry into Production. Confirm routine runtime paths do not depend on /tmp or development staging.

4. Verify provider resilience and unattended operation
Inspect real Symcor/Tungsten integration evidence, environment-specific endpoints, TLS/authentication, timeouts and error handling. Determine whether retries can duplicate Tungsten jobs: retrying the same endpoint after a timeout, 429 or 5xx is not inherently safe. Require documented idempotency or reconciliation behavior for ambiguous submissions; do not assume job-status/idempotency capabilities exist.
Review input -> processing -> completed -> archive transitions, incomplete-file handling, batch locking, crash recovery, duplicate processing and the approved scheduling interval. Confirm the exact AutoSys command, runtime account, working directory, configuration/secret loading, exit-code handling, logs/alerts and recovery runbook. Separate developer-account rehearsal from scheduler-account acceptance.
Inspect SIT/PAT, business acceptance, required scans, release/promotion approvals and Production host/account readiness against project requirements. Do not invent new release frameworks.

5. Perform safe verification and deliver the decision
Inspect test side effects before executing existing validation commands in an isolated workspace. Run appropriate application and deployment regression checks, including supported Linux/POSIX checks that Windows skipped. Record commands, exact candidate, results and reasons for skips; clearly label historical test reports. No live provider requests, shared-host installation, scheduler changes, sudoers changes, source/config edits, rebuild/publish, commit/push/merge or deployment are authorized by this assessment. Produce only the assessment report. If access is blocked, continue independent checks and state exactly what could not be verified.

Save CLUE_PRODUCTION_READINESS_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-01.md in the existing project documentation location. Lead with:
- GO: the exact candidate satisfies every mandatory Production requirement with evidence.
- NO_GO: a confirmed mandatory defect or unmet prerequisite prevents deployment.
- INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE: no confirmed blocker was established, but mandatory readiness evidence is missing. This does not authorize deployment.

Keep local tests, DEV host evidence, live-provider runs, SIT/PAT/business acceptance and Production operations separate. Provide:
Area | PASS / FAIL / UNVERIFIED / N/A | Candidate and evidence | Practical impact | Smallest next action

List confirmed blockers separately from missing evidence and optional improvements. For each blocker/gap, give the smallest corrective action, responsible role and evidence needed to close it. Finish with at most five prioritized actions and a direct answer to whether we can go to Production today. Do not claim readiness from test counts, screenshots, a DEV test artifact or a single successful CREDIT run alone.
